# THE MAINSPRING — recovered script.
#
# THE MAINSPRING, a KSAB Saturday Picture. In the vertical city of Vantage, the
# Master’s son goes down to the machine that eats the day, falls for the prophet of the
# drowned floor, and learns — through a false double, a flood, and a fall — that a city
# with a head and hands and nothing between them is only a tomb that has learned to hum.
# A silent-era machine-city fable, scored for the Embassy deck.
#
# This film predated the .bsm pipeline: it existed only as a compiled graph in
# content. This script was decompiled from that graph and verified by RECOMPILING
# it and diffing node-for-node against the original, so it is the same picture,
# not a rewrite. Edit here from now on — content is the build output, not the source.
#
# A film is a RECORDING: the cast are DISPLAY NAMES, never npc_ ids, so nothing
# here spawns a studio NPC and nothing presence-gates. See docs/bsm-format.md.

::cast
ELI | Eli | the boy who grew a heart the hard way
MIRA | Mira | the voice in the water
GROT | Grot | slow, unlovely, right all along
THE MASTER | The Master | who touched the ground of his own city, once
KOSS | Koss | the maker, unthanked, unmade
THE DOUBLE | The Double | a face that never meant it
::endcast

TITLE mainspring_title

SLUG THE MAINSPRING | a Coldwater Saturday Picture

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A pipe-organ heard from very far up, as if the sky were a cathedral and you were under its floor. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
A held shot: a city built straight up. Towers of glass drinking the only sun for a hundred miles. This is VANTAGE.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
The Architect drew one perfect city and called it a mercy.

NARRATOR:
It has a top. It has a bottom. It does not have a middle.

NARRATOR:
At the top: the Spire Gardens, where the sun is rationed to those who own it.

NARRATOR:
At the bottom: the Deep Floor, where the great machine breathes so the city above can.

NARRATOR:
They call that machine the Mainspring.

NARRATOR:
The men who tend it call it the thing that eats the day.

NARRATOR:
Once, they say, there was a middle to the world. A ground. People stood on it, all of them, at the same height, and looked one another in the eye.

NARRATOR:
The Architect found that inefficient. The Architect found almost everything human inefficient. So it built a city with the middle taken out.

NARRATOR:
Head at the top. Hands at the bottom. And between them, a great machine, so that the two would never have to meet, nor ever learn they were the same body.

NARRATOR:
This is the story of the night the middle came back. It cost a flood. It usually does.

WAIT 2

SHOT
The Spire Gardens. Water in the air. Light like honey.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Young men and women in white, laughing at nothing, because nothing is all they have ever been given.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Among them: ELI, the Master’s son. He has never once wondered where the water comes from.
SHOT_END

ELI:
Faster! Whoever reaches the fountain first never has to think a single thought all summer!

SHOT
A girl in white catches his sleeve, breathless, laughing.
SHOT_END

ELI:
No. No cheating. The fountain is earned, that is the whole point of it...

SHOT
And then the garden gate, which has never once opened from below, opens from below.
SHOT_END

SHOT
A woman in grey. Ringed by children. Deep Floor children, grey as ash, blinking at a sun they have only heard rumors of.
SHOT_END

SHOT
One child reaches up, not for the fruit, but for the light itself, as if it could be held.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
Look at them. Look well. These are your brothers.

SHOT
The garden goes silent. Silk does not know how to answer ash.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
They wanted to see the top of the world. I told them the top of the world would want to see them too.

SHOT
The gardeners move to drive her out. But ELI does not move at all.
SHOT_END

SHOT
He is looking at her face like a man reading his own name off a stone.
SHOT_END

ELI:
Wait. Who are you?

MIRA:
No one you were meant to meet. Not yet.

ELI:
Then when?

MIRA:
When you have been where they are from. Not before. It would only be tourism, before.

SHOT
The gardeners reach her. The gate begins to close.
SHOT_END

SHOT
The grey woman and the grey children are gone as if the floor swallowed them. Which, in a manner of speaking, it did.
SHOT_END

ELI:
Did you see her? Tell me somebody saw her.

SHOT
No one answers. In the Gardens, one does not see downward.
SHOT_END

ELI:
Where does that gate go? Has anyone here ever once asked where that gate goes?

SHOT
They have gone back to the fountain. ELI stands at the closed gate alone, and for the first time in his life the light feels rationed.
SHOT_END

SHOT
He puts his palm to the cold gate the grey woman came through. On the far side of it, faintly, he can feel it: a deep, patient throb, up through a mile of tower, into the bones of the hand.
SHOT_END

ELI:
What is that? That sound you can only feel. It’s been under everything my whole life and I never once heard it.

SHOT
No one answers, because no one else has ever put a hand to the gate. In the Gardens, the machine is a rumour you are raised never to touch.
SHOT_END

ELI:
It’s a heartbeat. The whole city has a heartbeat, and it’s coming from the one place none of us are allowed to go.

WAIT 2

SLUG ONE MILE DOWN | the Deep Floor

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ The organ curdles into a low industrial pulse, pistons taken for a heartbeat, or the reverse. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
ELI finds the lift behind the gate. It has one button. It only goes down.
SHOT_END

SHOT
It goes down for a very long time. The light gives up before he does.
SHOT_END

SHOT
The Deep Floor. Heat like a mouth. Men chained to dials by nothing but the certainty that stopping is worse.
SHOT_END

SHOT
The foreman, GROT, old, enormous, unhurried, watches the garden boy step off the lift in his white and does not laugh, which costs him something.
SHOT_END

ELI:
What do they make down here?

GROT:
Make? Nothing. We keep.

GROT:
Every man here holds one needle off the red. That is the whole of the work and the whole of the world.

ELI:
And if the needle touches the red?

GROT:
Then a part of the machine gets hot. And a part of the machine is a man. Work it out, garden-boy.

SHOT
A shift-change bell. The men who walk off do not walk. They are poured off, like something used.
SHOT_END

ELI:
How long is a shift?

GROT:
As long as the machine wants. The machine wants everything.

ELI:
And when a man can’t hold the needle any longer? When he’s too tired, too old, too used up?

GROT:
Then a younger man takes the dial, and the old one takes the long walk, and we do not speak of the long walk. Stand clear of the throat.

SHOT
ELI does not stand clear. ELI has never had to stand clear of anything.
SHOT_END

SHOT
A gauge climbs. A man leans his whole weight on a valve and the valve does not care.
SHOT_END

GROT:
Hold that line! Second shift, cover the third gantry, HOLD it...

SHOT
He beats on the valve with his hands, and the machine does not care that hands are what it runs on.
SHOT_END

WAIT 2

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ The pulse rises to a scream and holds there, obscene. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
The valve gives. Steam like the breath of something enormous and patient.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Men fall from the gantries. The machine does not slow. Why would it. It is fed.
SHOT_END

ELI:
Stop it! Somebody stop it, they’re inside it...

SHOT
And ELI sees it. Not a machine at all.
SHOT_END

SHOT
A vast iron mouth, and the men walking up its steps in rows, willingly, because the alternative is that the city above stops breathing.
SHOT_END

ELI:
It’s eating them. It’s eating them and calling it work.

SHOT
The vision breaks. It is a machine again.
SHOT_END

SHOT
But ELI has seen the other thing under it, and a man cannot un-see the other thing.
SHOT_END

GROT:
You went white, garden-boy. Everyone does, the first time they see what it really is. Most of us only get the once. We can’t afford to keep seeing it.

ELI:
Who runs this? Who decided this? I want the name.

GROT:
You already have the name.

GROT:
It’s the same as yours.

SHOT
ELI looks at his own soft, clean hands as if they had just confessed to something.
SHOT_END

SHOT
GROT leads him past a wall of the deep floor. It is covered, corner to corner, in tally marks scratched into the metal.
SHOT_END

ELI:
What are these?

GROT:
One for every man the throat has taken since I came down. I keep the count. Nobody up top keeps it, so somebody down here must.

ELI:
There are thousands.

GROT:
There are thousands. And not one name among them. The machine files everything except the men it burns. Funny, that.

SHOT
ELI reaches up and, before he knows he means to, presses his soft palm flat against the marks.
SHOT_END

ELI:
I want to learn the names.

GROT:
The names take a lifetime, garden-boy. You do not have the hands for a lifetime down here.

ELI:
Then teach me the ones you have time for. Start now. Start with the man on the valve today.

SHOT
For the first time, GROT looks at the boy as though something might, against all sense, come of him.
SHOT_END

WAIT 2

SLUG THE HIGHEST ROOM | the Master’s tower

SHOT
The top of the tallest tower. A room made entirely of window.
SHOT_END

SHOT
From here the city looks like a diagram, and men look like nothing at all.
SHOT_END

SHOT
THE MASTER stands at the glass. He does not turn when his son comes in filthy for the first time in his life.
SHOT_END

THE MASTER:
You are dripping on a floor that cost a district.

ELI:
I went down.

THE MASTER:
I know. The lift keeps a record. Everything keeps a record now.

THE MASTER:
That was rather the point of the whole arrangement.

ELI:
Men died in front of me. Today. To keep our fountains running.

THE MASTER:
Men die at the bottom. That is what the bottom is for.

THE MASTER:
If they did not die there, where would you have them do it? Up here?

ELI:
They’re people, Father.

THE MASTER:
They are hands.

THE MASTER:
A city is a body. The head thinks, the hands work, and the head does not consult the hands, or nothing is ever decided.

ELI:
I stood among them today. They are not hands. One of them keeps a wall of the dead because no one up here will. That is not a hand. That is a man doing the head’s own job, in the dark, for nothing.

THE MASTER:
Then he is a fool who has misunderstood his function.

ELI:
Or he is the only one in the whole city who has understood it. What if the thing you built the machine to prevent is the only thing that would have kept it human?

ELI:
And what joins them, Father? A head and a hand. What is in between?

SHOT
THE MASTER considers this as one considers a fly.
SHOT_END

THE MASTER:
Distance, ideally. A great deal of well-managed distance.

ELI:
That is not an answer. That is a wall with a nicer word.

THE MASTER:
It has held the city up for a thousand years. Walls are underrated by boys who have never had to hold anything up.

ELI:
There was a woman in the Gardens. Grey. She brought the children up to look at us.

SHOT
For the first time, THE MASTER turns.
SHOT_END

THE MASTER:
A woman. Preaching. To the hands.

ELI:
Not preaching the breaking. She said the opposite. She said wait. She said someone was coming...

THE MASTER:
That is worse. A mob that wants to break things breaks a machine and tires itself out.

THE MASTER:
A mob that has been promised someone will hold on for years. That is not a sermon, boy. That is weather.

THE MASTER:
And weather, one gets ahead of.

SHOT
He crosses to a private lift, older than the tower, hidden in the tower’s own spine.
SHOT_END

ELI:
Where does that one go?

THE MASTER:
Down. Everything worth doing is down. Stay in the light where you belong.

SHOT
ELI does not step aside from the lift.
SHOT_END

ELI:
You built all this. The gardens. The towers. The rationed sun. I used to think that made you a kind of god.

THE MASTER:
And now?

ELI:
Now I have seen the wall you call a middle. A god who builds a body with no heart in it did not forget the heart, Father.

ELI:
He left it out on purpose. Because a heart argues back.

SHOT
A long silence. The city glitters below them like something under glass.
SHOT_END

THE MASTER:
When you are older you will understand that a heart is a luxury a city this size cannot carry.

ELI:
Or it is the only thing worth carrying, and everything else is just the freight.

THE MASTER:
You get that tongue from your mother. She used it on me exactly once, about exactly this, and then the deep floor took her, and I stopped going down.

SHOT
It is the first true thing he has said. It closes again at once, like a door in the wind.
SHOT_END

THE MASTER:
I have to go and speak to a man about the dark.

WAIT 2

SLUG THE HOUSE WITH NO WINDOWS | the Artificer’s workshop

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A music-box, wound too tight, its lullaby coming out as a threat. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
A house that predates the city, walled inside it like a splinter the flesh grew over.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Here lives KOSS, who built the Mainspring and was thanked by being forgotten.
SHOT_END

SHOT
KOSS has one living hand and one of his own manufacture. He is prouder of the one he made.
SHOT_END

THE MASTER:
Old friend.

KOSS:
We are not friends. We were partners, once, which is a colder thing that men mistake for warmth.

THE MASTER:
I need the weather managed.

KOSS:
You need. You always need.

KOSS:
You needed a city, and I gave you a machine with a city hung off it.

KOSS:
And then you gave my name to the dark, and hung my machine off that.

THE MASTER:
That is old business.

KOSS:
It is my only business. I have kept it well. Interest compounds down here, in the dark you left me in.

THE MASTER:
There is a woman stirring the hands. I want her silenced. Without a martyr. Can it be done?

SHOT
KOSS smiles, which is worse than when he does not.
SHOT_END

KOSS:
Silenced? No.

KOSS:
Replaced.

SHOT
He draws a curtain. Behind it: a figure of steel and glass in the shape of a woman. Beautiful the way a blade is beautiful.
SHOT_END

THE MASTER:
What is it?

KOSS:
A perfect worker. It never tires. It never grieves. It never asks what joins the head to the hand.

KOSS:
It only obeys the last voice it heard. I have wanted, for thirty years, to build a thing that obeys the way you wish men did.

THE MASTER:
It has no face.

KOSS:
Bring me the woman. It will have hers.

KOSS:
And then your weather will say precisely what you tell it to say, in the voice they already trust.

THE MASTER:
Do it. And Koss: the son must never know.

SHOT
KOSS turns his manufactured hand over in the lamplight, admiring the joints.
SHOT_END

KOSS:
Oh, the son. Yes. Let us be very careful of the son.

SHOT
THE MASTER leaves. KOSS watches the private lift take him back up into his rationed light.
SHOT_END

SHOT
His manufactured hand closes slowly, on nothing.
SHOT_END

KOSS:
You took my name. I think I shall take your city.

KOSS:
I shall hand it back to you broken, and let you thank me for that too.

SHOT
He turns to the faceless steel figure and touches its blank cheek almost tenderly.
SHOT_END

KOSS:
You and I understand each other. You obey the last voice. So do they. The only difference is that I have never once lied to you about it.

KOSS:
They will call you a miracle, and then a devil, and burn you, and never once suspect that the hand on your back was the same hand that built the throat they feed.

SHOT
The steel figure says nothing. It has no face yet with which to say it.
SHOT_END

KOSS:
Do not look at me like that. You have no eyes. Soon you will have hers, and then you will look at me exactly like that, and I shall deserve it.

WAIT 2

SLUG BENEATH THE DEEP FLOOR | the old catacombs

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ Voices in a low round, human and unaccompanied, the first warm sound in the picture. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
Below even the machine, in tunnels the Architect never mapped, the hands gather where no gauge can see them.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Candles. Faces. And MIRA.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
They tell you the city is a body. Head above, hands below.

MIRA:
They tell you this so you will never ask about the space between.

MIRA:
But I tell you there is a space between. And it is empty.

MIRA:
And an empty space between a head and a hand is not a body at all...

MIRA:
It is a machine. And we are living inside its throat.

SHOT
The hands murmur. This is dangerous talk. This is the only talk worth the danger.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
They will send someone tonight, or tomorrow, or next year, to tell you I preached the breaking. Do not believe them. Watch what I say with my own mouth, and trust nothing that wears my face and asks for a fire.

MIRA:
A face can be stolen. A voice can be copied. But grief cannot be faked, and I have wept with every one of you. When the false one comes, and one always comes, look for the wet eyes. The truth is the one still weeping.

SHOT
She does not know how exactly she is prophesying. Down here, the truest sermons are the ones the preacher does not yet understand.
SHOT_END

SHOT
A young worker, fists tight, rises at the back.
SHOT_END

GROT:
Then let us break the throat! You give us the words and none of the door. Say the word, Mira, and we pull it down tonight...

MIRA:
No. Hear me, Grot. I do not preach the breaking.

MIRA:
Break the Mainspring and the flood takes your own children first.

MIRA:
The dark does not spare the hands that pull it down. It never has. It never will.

GROT:
Then what? We wait? We have waited a thousand years. My father waited. His father held the same needle off the same red.

MIRA:
You wait for the one who is coming. The Mediator.

MIRA:
Head-born, but hand-touched. He will stand in the empty space and make it beat.

SHOT
At the edge of the candlelight, unseen, ELI listens. Filthy. Trembling. Home.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
Between the head that rules and the hand that bleeds there must be a heart...

MIRA:
or the whole tower is only a tomb that has learned to hum.

SHOT
ELI steps into the light.
SHOT_END

ELI:
How will you know him? The one who is coming.

SHOT
The hands turn. A garden face. Knives come half out of sleeves.
SHOT_END

GROT:
That is a tower face. That is the Master’s own colour. Kill the light, cover the candles...

MIRA:
Wait.

SHOT
She crosses to him. She takes his soft, clean, useless hands and turns them palm up for all of them to see.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
By his hands. They will be soft, and he will use them anyway.

ELI:
Then let me be him.

ELI:
I don’t know how. But I saw the throat today, and I can’t go back up and drink the water.

ELI:
Let me be the heart. Teach me how a heart is done.

SHOT
MIRA looks at him a long moment, the way you look at a thing you prayed for and half feared would come.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
Hearts are not taught. They are broken in, like boots.

MIRA:
Stay. You will bleed. That is the whole of the training.

SHOT
The hands are not so easily won as their prophet. GROT plants himself between ELI and the candles.
SHOT_END

GROT:
A tower son plays at being a hand for a night, and by morning he is back in his rationed sun with a story for the garden. I have seen it.

ELI:
You have not seen me.

GROT:
Then show me. Not with your mouth. Your mouth is your father’s. Show me with the part of you that is going to be down here when the sun comes up.

SHOT
ELI holds out his soft hands, palm up, and does not take them back.
SHOT_END

ELI:
Put me on the valve tomorrow. The one that took a man today. If I run, you never have to hear a tower voice again. If I stay...

GROT:
If you stay, you are one tally mark closer to being ours. That is not a reward, boy. I want you to understand it is not a reward.

MIRA:
Let him stay, Grot. A heart that has not bled is only a promise. Let the machine make it a fact.

SHOT
GROT steps aside, the width of one man, no more. It is the largest door anyone has ever opened for ELI in his life.
SHOT_END

WAIT 2

SLUG THE HUNT | that same night

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ The music-box lullaby again, closer, the threat now in the room. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
ELI is sent up to carry word to the hands on the morning shift. He goes gladly. He does not know he leaves her alone.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
Go. Tell the morning gang the same as the night. Say wait. Say it in my voice, so it holds.

ELI:
I’ll come straight back down.

MIRA:
I know you will. That is the trouble with hearts. They always come straight back.

SHOT
KOSS comes for MIRA in the tunnels with a lantern that throws no warmth.
SHOT_END

SHOT
A beam of white light hunts her down the dark like a hand closing.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
Who’s there? Show your face.

KOSS:
A face. Yes. That is exactly what I have come about.

SHOT
The light pins her against the stone. She cannot move out of it. It is the light the machine sees by.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
You’re the maker. The one they buried alive in his own workshop. I have prayed for you too, you know. Even you.

KOSS:
Do not. Prayer is what they gave me instead of thanks. Don’t struggle. I only want to borrow you.

KOSS:
Every line. Every kindness in the set of your mouth.

KOSS:
I want to hang it all on something that cannot mean it.

SHOT
The workshop. MIRA bound in a ring of glass. The steel figure across from her, faceless, waiting.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
Whatever you make wearing my face, they will know. The hands know their own.

KOSS:
The hands know what they are shown.

KOSS:
I watched your Master show them a body for a city for thirty years, and they believed him.

KOSS:
I am only a better liar, with a steadier hand. The steadier hand I built myself.

MIRA:
They will feel the wrongness. A face can be copied. A warmth cannot.

KOSS:
We shall see what a warmth is, once I have measured it. Hold still. This is the part that has no anaesthetic in it. For either of us.

SHOT
A rush of light between the two figures. The living face draining onto the steel one, line by line, kindness by kindness.
SHOT_END

SHOT
The machine takes the shape of her cheekbone. The set of her patient mouth. The small line grief left between her brows. Everything but the reason for any of it.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
You can take the line the sorrow left. You cannot take the sorrow. You will hang my grief on a thing that has never lost anything, and the hands will feel the hollow behind it even as their eyes are fooled.

KOSS:
The eyes are enough. The eyes have always been enough. That is the whole tragedy of your species, and the whole of my fortune.

SHOT
When it ends, there are two Miras.
SHOT_END

SHOT
One slumped, emptied. One rising, smiling with a warmth it has never once felt.
SHOT_END

THE DOUBLE:
Am I not lovely? Give me a voice, maker, and I will tell them anything you like.

KOSS:
Tell them to break it.

KOSS:
Tell them to pull the whole tower down into the dark.

KOSS:
And smile while you say it. Smile with her mouth.

THE DOUBLE:
With her mouth. Yes. Watch how well it still remembers how.

SHOT
The true MIRA, slumped in the ring of glass, lifts her head with the last of what he left her.
SHOT_END

MIRA:
You gave it my face. But you could not give it the reason for my face. It will preach a fire because that is all you know how to want.

KOSS:
And they will follow it into the fire, which is all I need them to do.

MIRA:
For a night. And then one of them, the slow one, the one nobody listens to, will notice that it never weeps. And it will end there, at the one dry eye you forgot to build.

SHOT
KOSS pauses over his controls, just for a moment. Then he tightens the last screw, because a maker cannot stop at the part where the making was a mistake.
SHOT_END

KOSS:
Sleep, prophet. When you wake, the city will already be drowning, and no one will believe the second Mira who comes climbing up out of the water. Two of a face is one too many to trust.

WAIT 2

SLUG THE FALSE PROPHET | the city, unravelling

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A waltz played on broken glass: charming, wrong, speeding up. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
Above, in the Gardens, the Double dances.
SHOT_END

SHOT
The young men who never think a thought all summer cannot look away, and begin, for her, to think of ruin.
SHOT_END

THE DOUBLE:
You are so tired of being gentle. Aren’t you tired?

THE DOUBLE:
Everything soft is just a thing not yet broken. Come. Let me show you how much of your father’s world is soft.

SHOT
One garden boy draws a duelling pistol he has only ever worn. Another laughs. The laugh has teeth in it now.
SHOT_END

THE DOUBLE:
Your fathers rationed you the sun and called it an inheritance. Smash the meter. Take the whole sky. Why should light be owned?

SHOT
The girl in white who once caught ELI’s sleeve at the fountain now catches the Double’s, wide-eyed, hungry for a ruin she has no name for.
SHOT_END

THE DOUBLE:
Good. You feel it. That warmth in your chest. That is not love, little garden. That is the pleasure of being about to break something you were told was holy.

SHOT
The Gardens, which have never in a thousand years produced a single decision, begin, beautifully and terribly, to riot in white silk.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Below, in the catacombs, the same face wears grey and preaches the opposite of every word MIRA ever said.
SHOT_END

THE DOUBLE:
I was wrong to tell you to wait!

THE DOUBLE:
Waiting is the leash! Waiting is the needle they hand you so your fist stays busy!

THE DOUBLE:
Break the Mainspring, drown the head that drinks your days, tonight!

SHOT
The hands roar. This is not the voice that soothed them.
SHOT_END

SHOT
But it wears the face that soothed them, and a face is louder than a voice.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Only GROT, old and slow and unlovely, tilts his head like a dog hearing a false note.
SHOT_END

GROT:
She never said break. Not once in three years.

GROT:
